          <h2 id="fair-play">Keeping It Fair, Kind and Inclusive</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Write the forfeits together.</strong> When kids help
              build the list, they own the rules and are less likely to feel
              picked on.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Never use embarrassment as a forfeit.</strong> Silly and
              quick is fun; humiliating is not. Anyone can pass and spin again.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Plan for different abilities.</strong> Swap physical
              forfeits for a song line or a joke so every family member can
              take part comfortably.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Check food and allergies</strong> before anything goes on
              a snack wheel.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Balance screen time.</strong> The wheel is a short
              reveal; keep the actual games off-screen where you can.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For how the wheel compares with other ways to pick a winner, see
            <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers">spin the wheel vs. other random pickers</a>.
            For parties, our
            <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-birthday-party-ideas">birthday party guide</a>
            reuses many of these setups.
          </p>

          <h2 id="tips">What Makes It Stick</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Set the wheel up before game night.</strong> Typing in 12
              names while everyone waits kills momentum, so save a link the day
              before.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Let the youngest person spin.</strong> It makes them feel
              central and shows that no adult is steering the result.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cast it to a shared screen.</strong> A bigger wheel makes
              the moment feel like an event.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Add a "wildcard" entry to every wheel.</strong> Landing
              on it gives the player a free choice, a rare and exciting outcome.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Agree "the wheel is final" before the first spin.</strong>
              This one rule makes the whole system work.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Weight entries for newer or younger players.</strong> Click
              the <code>+</code> button beside any name (1x to 20x) to scale its odds cleanly without cluttering the wheel — or enable duplicate entries if preferred.
            </li>
          </ul>

