        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How many entries can I add to the wheel for game night?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Most family game nights use between 4 and 20 entries, depending
              on whether you are picking players, categories or games. The
              wheel resizes its slices automatically as you add more.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I use it on a TV screen during game night?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Yes. Cast your browser to a smart TV or connect a laptop by HDMI,
              then switch to fullscreen so the whole room can watch the spin
              together.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What if the same player keeps getting picked repeatedly?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Turn on remove after spin. Each selected entry comes off the
              wheel once picked, so everyone gets a turn before anyone repeats.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I give certain family members better odds?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Yes. Tap the <code>+</code> or <code>-</code> weight button beside any name to scale odds from 1x up to 20x without duplicating text — or enable duplicate entries. Setting a name to 2x doubles its slice width and odds, making it easy to balance turns for younger players.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How do I keep forfeits kind for young children?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Let the kids help write the forfeit list and keep it to silly,
              quick actions such as a funny walk, a song line or ten jumping
              jacks. Avoid anything embarrassing, and let any player pass and
              spin again.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Are there other wheel types worth using beyond the standard one?</summary>
            <div class="faq-a">
              Yes. A Yes or No wheel is a quick companion for binary calls such
              as whether to take a break, and a number picker or letter picker
              can power guessing and word rounds.
            </div>
          </details>

          <div class="box box-success">
            <div class="box-label">Key takeaways</div>
            <ul>
              <li>
                A wheel removes the fairness and sequencing arguments that
                quietly derail game night.
              </li>
              <li>
                It scales from toddlers spinning for snacks to adults running
                competitive trivia.
              </li>
              <li>
                The strongest setups use two wheels: one for players and one
                for category or forfeit.
              </li>
              <li>
                Save your wheels, keep forfeits kind, and agree "the wheel is
                final" before the first spin.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

